sections.body:
**Nginx** (pronounced "engine-x") is the most widely deployed web server in the world, found in front of the majority of high-traffic websites, APIs, and microservices. Unlike traditional servers such as Apache that create a new thread per connection, nginx uses an **event-driven, non-blocking I/O model**: a small number of worker processes each handle thousands of concurrent connections using an event loop, similar to Node.js. This architecture makes nginx extremely efficient under load — it consumes far less memory than thread-per-connection servers and degrades gracefully under high concurrency.

**Three roles in one binary:** nginx can serve as a static file server, a reverse proxy, and a load balancer, often performing all three simultaneously. In production, the most common pattern is nginx-as-reverse-proxy: nginx accepts all external traffic, terminates TLS, and forwards requests to backend application servers. The backends can be written in any language and never need to handle TLS themselves.

**Configuration structure:** nginx config is hierarchical. The top-level `nginx.conf` file defines global settings and loads the `http` block. Inside `http`, you define one or more `server` blocks — each `server` block is a virtual host, listening on a port and matching incoming requests by the `Host` header. Inside each `server`, `location` blocks define how specific URL patterns are handled: serve a static file, proxy to a backend, redirect, return an error, and so on. This three-level hierarchy (http → server → location) gives nginx its flexibility.

**Worker processes and connections:** `worker_processes auto` tells nginx to spawn one worker per CPU core. Each worker handles up to `worker_connections` simultaneous connections. Total concurrent connections = workers × connections. For a 4-core machine with `worker_connections 1024`, that is 4,096 simultaneous connections — sufficient for most workloads. Understanding this helps you tune nginx for high-traffic scenarios and explains why `worker_processes 1` in development containers is intentional.

**Reload without downtime:** nginx has a built-in mechanism for zero-downtime config reloads. When you run `nginx -s reload`, the master process reads the new config, validates it, and signals the existing workers to finish current requests and exit. New workers are spawned with the updated config. During this transition, both old and new workers are alive — no requests are dropped. This makes config changes safe to apply to production systems without causing even a single dropped connection.

Nginx is a high-performance web server, reverse proxy, and load balancer. Its event-driven architecture handles tens of thousands of concurrent connections efficiently.

**Nginx config structure:**

```nginx
# /etc/nginx/nginx.conf — main config
worker_processes auto;  # one worker per CPU core

events {
    worker_connections 1024;  # max connections per worker
}

http {
    include       /etc/nginx/mime.types;
    default_type  application/octet-stream;
    sendfile on;
    keepalive_timeout 65;

    # Include all site configs
    include /etc/nginx/conf.d/*.conf;
}
```

```nginx
# /etc/nginx/conf.d/myapp.conf — virtual host (server block)

# Serve static files
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com www.example.com;

    root /var/www/html;
    index index.html;

    # Cache static assets
    location ~* \.(js|css|png|jpg|ico)$ {
        expires 30d;
        add_header Cache-Control "public, immutable";
    }

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;  # SPA fallback
    }
}
```

```bash
# Test config syntax before reloading
nginx -t

# Reload config (no downtime)
nginx -s reload
# or via systemctl:
# systemctl reload nginx  # (systemd not in containers)

# Check nginx is running and listening
ps aux | grep nginx
ss -tlnp | grep ':80\|:443'

# View access and error logs
tail -f /var/log/nginx/access.log
tail -f /var/log/nginx/error.log
```

A **reverse proxy** is a server that sits in front of backend servers and forwards client requests to them. The defining characteristic is directionality: the proxy acts on behalf of the *server*, not the client. Clients connect to the proxy's IP and port; they have no knowledge of (and no direct access to) the backend servers. This architectural pattern is foundational to modern web infrastructure — CDNs, API gateways, Kubernetes Ingress controllers, and cloud load balancers are all reverse proxies.

**Why the reverse proxy pattern matters for DevOps:**

- **SSL/TLS termination:** the proxy handles the TLS handshake and certificate management. Backends receive plain HTTP, eliminating the need to configure TLS in each application. This centralizes certificate management and reduces CPU overhead on backend servers.
- **Security boundary:** backend servers are not exposed to the public internet. Their IPs, ports, and even their existence are hidden behind the proxy. A backend can be restarted, scaled, or replaced without any change to the client-facing address.
- **Request routing:** a single proxy can route different URL prefixes to different backend services — `/api/` goes to the Python API, `/` goes to the React frontend, `/grafana/` goes to Grafana. This enables clean URL namespacing without requiring backends to be aware of their URL prefix.
- **Header manipulation:** the proxy can add, remove, or modify HTTP headers before forwarding. The `X-Real-IP` and `X-Forwarded-For` headers tell backends the original client IP (since without these, every request appears to come from the proxy's IP). The `X-Forwarded-Proto` header tells backends whether the original request used HTTP or HTTPS.
- **WebSocket proxying:** nginx supports HTTP upgrade requests, enabling WebSocket connections through the proxy. This requires explicit configuration of the `Upgrade` and `Connection` headers — omitting these is the most common cause of WebSocket failures behind nginx.

**How Kubernetes Ingress relates:** the `ingress-nginx` controller runs nginx as a Deployment inside your cluster. Kubernetes `Ingress` resources are translated by the controller into nginx `server` and `location` blocks — annotations like `nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target` map directly to nginx directives. Understanding raw nginx config makes Ingress annotations immediately legible.

A reverse proxy sits in front of backend servers. Clients talk to the proxy, not the backends directly. This provides: security (backend IPs hidden), SSL termination, caching, and request routing.

```
Client → Nginx (reverse proxy) → Backend App (port 3000)
                               → Backend API (port 8000)
                               → Microservice (port 5000)
```

```nginx
# Reverse proxy to a backend app
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name app.example.com;

    location / {
        proxy_pass         http://127.0.0.1:3000;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;

        # Forward client information to backend
        proxy_set_header   Host              $host;
        proxy_set_header   X-Real-IP         $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-For   $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;

        # WebSocket support
        proxy_set_header   Upgrade    $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header   Connection 'upgrade';

        # Timeouts
        proxy_read_timeout    60s;
        proxy_connect_timeout 10s;
    }

    # Route /api to a different backend
    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000/;
    }
}
```

**SSL termination at the proxy (the standard pattern):**

```nginx
# HTTPS server — decrypt TLS here, forward plain HTTP to backend
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name app.example.com;

    ssl_certificate     /etc/nginx/ssl/app.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/ssl/app.key;
    ssl_protocols       TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
    ssl_ciphers         HIGH:!aNULL:!MD5;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;  # plain HTTP to backend
    }
}

# Redirect HTTP to HTTPS
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name app.example.com;
    return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
}
```

This is what Kubernetes Ingress controllers like `ingress-nginx` do: they run nginx and configure it based on `Ingress` resources.

**Load balancing** is the practice of distributing incoming network traffic across multiple backend instances so that no single instance is overwhelmed. It is one of the two primary mechanisms for horizontal scalability (the other being stateless application design). Without load balancing, adding more backend servers provides no benefit — all traffic still hits the same instance.

Load balancers operate at different OSI layers, and the layer determines what information is available for routing decisions. **Layer 4 (L4) load balancers** operate at the TCP level — they see source/destination IPs and ports, but nothing about the HTTP content. They are extremely fast and protocol-agnostic. AWS Network Load Balancer (NLB) is L4. **Layer 7 (L7) load balancers** operate at the HTTP level — they can inspect URLs, headers, cookies, and request bodies to make routing decisions. They can route `/api/` to one pool and `/static/` to another, inject headers, terminate TLS, and perform health checks on actual HTTP endpoints. AWS Application Load Balancer (ALB) and nginx are L7.

**Load balancing algorithms** determine which backend receives each request:

- **Round-robin** (nginx default): requests are distributed sequentially across backends. Simple and effective when backends are homogeneous and requests have similar cost.
- **Least connections** (`least_conn`): each new request goes to the backend with the fewest active connections. Better than round-robin when requests have highly variable processing time — long-running requests don't starve other backends.
- **IP hash** (`ip_hash`): a hash of the client's IP determines which backend handles all requests from that IP. This provides **session affinity** (also called "sticky sessions") — essential for applications that store session state in memory rather than a shared database. Note that IP hash distributes poorly when many clients share a NAT IP.
- **Weighted round-robin**: backends have weights, and higher-weight backends receive proportionally more traffic. Used during canary deployments (send 5% of traffic to new version) or when backends have different hardware capacities.

**Health checks** are critical: a load balancer must stop sending traffic to backends that are down. Nginx Open Source supports passive health checks only (it detects failures after they happen, by observing failed responses). Nginx Plus and cloud load balancers support active health checks (periodic probes to a health endpoint). In Kubernetes, **readiness probes** perform this function — a pod that fails its readiness probe is removed from the Service's endpoint list, which removes it from the load balancer's rotation.

**Session affinity and statelessness:** the cleanest approach is to design applications to be completely stateless — session data lives in a shared Redis or database, so any backend can serve any request. Stateless applications work perfectly with round-robin and recover from backend failures without dropping sessions. If you find yourself relying on IP hash or sticky sessions, treat it as a signal that the application needs architectural work.

A load balancer distributes incoming traffic across multiple backend instances. This provides horizontal scalability and high availability.

```nginx
# Define an upstream group with multiple backends
upstream backend_pool {
    # Round-robin (default): requests distributed evenly
    server 10.0.0.1:8080;
    server 10.0.0.2:8080;
    server 10.0.0.3:8080;

    # Health check (nginx plus / or use backup)
    server 10.0.0.4:8080 backup;  # only used if others are down
    server 10.0.0.5:8080 down;    # temporarily disabled
}

# Least connections: send to server with fewest active connections
upstream backend_pool {
    least_conn;
    server 10.0.0.1:8080;
    server 10.0.0.2:8080;
}

# IP hash: same client always goes to same backend (session affinity)
upstream backend_pool {
    ip_hash;
    server 10.0.0.1:8080;
    server 10.0.0.2:8080;
}

# Weighted: server 1 gets 3x more traffic than server 2
upstream backend_pool {
    server 10.0.0.1:8080 weight=3;
    server 10.0.0.2:8080 weight=1;
}

# Use the upstream in a server block
server {
    listen 80;
    location / {
        proxy_pass http://backend_pool;
    }
}
```

**Cloud load balancers follow the same concepts:**

```
AWS:    Elastic Load Balancer (ALB = L7, NLB = L4)
GCP:    Cloud Load Balancing
Azure:  Azure Load Balancer / Application Gateway

Kubernetes Service types:
  ClusterIP    — internal cluster LB (default)
  NodePort     — expose on node IP + port
  LoadBalancer — provision cloud LB (AWS ELB, GCP LB)
```

The best way to understand nginx configuration is to read and modify a working config, then observe the results. In this exercise, you will examine how the sandbox itself uses nginx as a reverse proxy, then design a configuration for a multi-service setup. This mirrors the real-world task you will face whenever you need to expose a new internal service or add a new routing rule.

Pay attention to the relationship between the `upstream` block and the `location` block — the upstream defines the pool of backend servers, while the location defines the routing rule that routes requests to that pool. Notice how the `proxy_set_header` directives pass metadata from nginx to the backend. When debugging a proxy configuration, always check nginx's error log first (`tail /var/log/nginx/error.log`) — it will tell you whether nginx couldn't connect to the backend, returned an error from the backend, or encountered a config syntax issue.

```bash norun
# The sandbox frontend is served by nginx which proxies to the backend
# Let's look at the nginx config that's running:

# The nginx.conf is inside the frontend container
docker exec devops-learning-lab-frontend-1 cat /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf
# (Run this from outside the container on your host machine)
# Or look at the file in the repo:
cat /workspace/../nginx.conf 2>/dev/null || echo 'Check frontend/nginx.conf'

# Inside the sandbox, test the reverse proxy behavior:
# The backend API is at port 8000
curl http://localhost:8000/api/lessons | python3 -m json.tool | head -20

# The frontend at port 3000 proxies /api/ to the backend
# (from outside: curl http://localhost:3000/api/lessons)
```

**Write a nginx config for a hypothetical 3-service setup:**

```nginx
# nginx-multi.conf
# Services:
#   web-app:    127.0.0.1:3000  (frontend SPA)
#   api:        127.0.0.1:8000  (backend API)
#   grafana:    127.0.0.1:3001  (monitoring)

upstream web { server 127.0.0.1:3000; }
upstream api  { server 127.0.0.1:8000; }
upstream grafana { server 127.0.0.1:3001; }

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name devops-lab.local;

    # Route /api/ to the backend
    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://api/;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
    }

    # Route /grafana/ to grafana
    location /grafana/ {
        proxy_pass http://grafana/;
    }

    # Everything else to the frontend
    location / {
        proxy_pass http://web;
        # SPA fallback
        proxy_intercept_errors on;
        error_page 404 = /index.html;
    }
}
```

```bash norun
# Validate the config syntax:
nginx -t -c /workspace/nginx-multi.conf 2>/dev/null || \
  docker run --rm -v /workspace/nginx-multi.conf:/etc/nginx/conf.d/test.conf nginx nginx -t
```
